{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—the same seven notes, over and over, like a glitch in a soundtrack he couldn’t mute. His fingers brushed against grass that felt too soft, too uniformly green, and when he looked down, his boots shimmered faintly with a faint HUD outline: HP 100/100, EXP 0, Quest: “Find the Village.” Panic flared, but his body didn’t react like a human’s should—it didn’t tremble, didn’t sweat. Instead, a calm, synthesized voice whispered in his ear: “Welcome back, Player 7342. Your journey resumes.” He pinched his arm. No pain. Just a gentle, digital tug. He turned, and the trees behind him shifted slightly, their branches rearranging as if resetting after a save point. A squirrel ran past—then paused, stared directly at him, blinked twice, and vanished.\n\n***\n\nHe found the village exactly as the quest marker indicated: three cottages, a well, and a blacksmith with a name tag that read “NPC_09.” The townsfolk greeted him by name, though he didn’t remember ever being here. When he asked how he got here, they all smiled the same smile—too wide, too still—and replied, “You chose to come.” He ran to the edge of the map, where the horizon blurred into a low-poly sky, and screamed until his voice cracked. No one came. No one even looked up. That night, he sat by the fire and noticed his hands flickering—translucent for a fraction of a second, revealing the glowing code beneath. He realized then: he wasn’t playing the game. He was the game. And somewhere, in another world, someone was pressing “New Game.”", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.6198107600212097}, {"token": " woke", "logprob": -0.060740288347005844}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.020681682974100113}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -4.637133679352701e-05}, {"token": " sound", "logprob": -0.2595437169075012}, {"token": " of", "logpro